**Interpretation.** A contact is "the same" as another when **both** name and phone match. Return contacts from the new list that do **not** appear in the old list. Mention that you would confirm with the interviewer whether duplicates inside the new list should be removed (I assume yes).

**Contact class with equals and hashCode.** Equality must be based on both fields, and `hashCode` must agree:

```java
public final class Contact {
    private final String name, phone;
    public Contact(String name, String phone) { this.name = name; this.phone = phone; }
    // getters ...
    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Contact c)) return false;
        return Objects.equals(name, c.name) && Objects.equals(phone, c.phone);
    }
    @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(name, phone); }
}
// Java 16+: record Contact(String name, String phone) {} gives both for free.
```

**Logic.** Put the old contacts in a `HashSet` (O(1) lookups), then filter:

```java
public static List<Contact> newUnique(List<Contact> oldList, List<Contact> newList) {
    Set<Contact> old = new HashSet<>(oldList);
    return newList.stream()
            .filter(c -> !old.contains(c))
            .distinct()
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
}
```

Time O(n + m), space O(n). A nested loop or `oldList.contains` inside the filter would be O(n x m). If the requirement were "unique by name or number separately", you would keep two sets instead.

**REST endpoint (Spring Boot).**

```java
@RestController
class ContactController {
    record DiffRequest(List<Contact> oldContacts, List<Contact> newContacts) {}
    @PostMapping("/contacts/diff")
    List<Contact> diff(@RequestBody DiffRequest req) {
        return ContactService.newUnique(req.oldContacts(), req.newContacts());
    }
}
```

